#
# Every source holding a string a user of this project reads, for the
# `evolution-jmap` gettext domain. `xgettext` reads this list and nothing else,
# so a marked string in an unlisted file is a string no translator is ever
# offered; `rust/crates/jmap-backend-core/tests/potfiles.rs` holds the list to
# the sources in both directions so that cannot happen quietly.
#
# Paths are relative to the directory holding this `po/`, which is gettext's
# convention and what the extraction has to be run from. `po/extract.sh` runs
# it, with the arguments that produced the committed `po/evolution-jmap.pot`;
# run that script after touching a translatable string.
#
# `-L C` because gettext 0.21 has no Rust parser. It extracts our markers
# correctly — `N_(c"…")` reads as a call with a string argument either way — but
# it lexes a lifetime as an opening character constant and says so:
#
#   provider.rs:125: warning: unterminated character constant
#
# That is not cosmetic. The lexer gives up at the end of the line, so a marked
# string sharing a line with an odd number of apostrophes is dropped from the
# catalogue outright — measured, not feared: `const NAME: &'static CStr =
# N_(c"JMAP");` extracts nothing but that warning. Which is why the committed
# `.pot` is compared against these sources in both directions rather than
# trusted; the check is in the test file named above, and it catches the rest
# of the class this instance belongs to as well.
#
# `-L C` costs one more thing, which the test file above also enforces: a
# marked literal must be written on a single line. A backslash at the end of a
# line means something slightly different in each language — Rust drops the
# newline and the indentation after it, C drops only the newline — so a wrapped
# message is extracted with the source's indentation inside it and can never
# match the msgid the program looks up. Long lines are the price of that; do not
# "tidy" one by wrapping it.
#
# What is NOT here, and why:
#
# - `src/*.c`. The C example module is upstream skeleton code from the GNOME
#   Evolution wiki, and it carries its own gettext domain — the top-level
#   CMakeLists.txt still sets `GETTEXT_PACKAGE` to `example-module`, which is
#   not this project's domain. Its strings ("My Maildir Folder Action…") are
#   demonstration text that will leave with the skeleton; folding them into
#   this catalogue would hand translators strings for a module nobody ships.
# - Anything under a crate's `tests/`. A string that exists only in a test
#   binary is not one a user reads.
# - Protocol constants, JMAP property names, log and trace text, and errors
#   only a developer sees. The standing directive in `docs/ROADMAP.md` excludes
#   them deliberately: translating them makes bug reports unreadable and gains
#   no user anything.

rust/crates/jmap-backend-cal/src/ops.rs
rust/crates/jmap-backend-core/src/connect.rs
rust/crates/jmap-backend-core/src/oauth2.rs
rust/crates/jmap-backend-collection/src/create_resource.rs
rust/crates/jmap-backend-collection/src/delete_resource.rs
rust/crates/jmap-config/src/backend.rs
rust/crates/jmap-config/src/complete.rs
rust/crates/jmap-config/src/config_lookup.rs
rust/crates/jmap-config/src/oauth2_service.rs
rust/crates/jmap-config/src/oauth2_setup.rs
rust/crates/jmap-mail/src/provider.rs
rust/crates/jmap-mail/src/sasl.rs
